@node Embedding
@chapter Embedding

libdtfl is built to run inside a host program, such as a database
server, that supplies its own memory, error reporting, logging and
threads.  The four host hooks of @ref{Error handling} route each of
those boundaries through the host.  This chapter shows the whole
embedding, using PostgreSQL as the example host.

@section Wiring the boundaries

A host installs the hooks once, before starting any computation.  Each
hook is a function the host provides plus an opaque context pointer.

@example
static void *pg_malloc(size_t n, void *c) @{ return palloc(n); @}
static void *pg_realloc(void *p, size_t n, void *c)
@{ return repalloc(p, n); @}
static void pg_free(void *p, void *c) @{ pfree(p); @}

/* longjmp out of the computation; never returns */
static void pg_panic(const char *msg, void *c)
@{ ereport(ERROR, errmsg("dtfl: %s", msg)); @}

static void pg_log(dtfl_log_level lvl, const char *msg, void *c)
@{ int e[] = @{ DEBUG1, LOG, WARNING, ERROR @}; elog(e[lvl], "%s", msg); @}

dtfl_alloc_hooks ah = @{ pg_malloc, pg_realloc, pg_free, NULL @};
dtfl_set_alloc_hooks(&ah);
dtfl_set_panic_hook(pg_panic, NULL);
dtfl_set_log_hook(pg_log, NULL);
dtfl_set_thread_hooks(&bgworker_hooks);  /* or drive standalone */
@end example

The log levels map straight onto the host's own: @code{DTFL_LOG_DEBUG},
@code{DTFL_LOG_INFO}, @code{DTFL_LOG_WARN} and @code{DTFL_LOG_ERROR}
become @code{DEBUG1}, @code{LOG}, @code{WARNING} and @code{ERROR}.  The
panic hook is expected to @code{longjmp} (via @code{ereport(ERROR)}) out
of the computation rather than return.

@section Hosts that control their own threads

A host that cannot hand libdtfl free-running threads has two ways to
embed it.

First, it can supply thread hooks whose @code{spawn} is backed by the
host's own worker mechanism (a PostgreSQL background worker, for
example).  @code{dtfl_execute} then starts and joins each worker through
those hooks, and the rest of the program is unchanged.

Second, and simplest when the host already has an event loop, it can
avoid @code{dtfl_execute} entirely and drive a single worker
cooperatively.  No thread is created, so no thread hook is needed; the
host calls @code{dtfl_worker_step} from its own thread whenever it
chooses, interleaving dataflow progress with its other work:

@example
dtfl_worker *w = dtfl_worker_new_standalone();
dtfl_worker_dataflow(w, "my", tc, build, ctx);
while (dtfl_worker_has_dataflows(w))
        dtfl_worker_step(w);
dtfl_worker_free(w);
@end example

@section What libdtfl never does

libdtfl installs no signal handlers and calls no @code{getenv},
@code{signal} or @code{getopt}.  When a host wants environment lookup, it
passes a callback to @code{dtfl_config_from_env}; the library never
reads the environment itself.  This keeps libdtfl safe to load into a
host that owns those facilities.  The complete contract, including the
per-thread ownership rules, is in @code{dtfl_embedding(7)} and
@code{dtfl(7)}.
